rota lap 4625, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes (hand-advanced: 4625 mod 5 is row 0, read today at lap 4609, and rows 1, 2 and 4 were read today too, so the least-read row took the lap). Its fixed seat, the happy zone and the thin edge, holds the collaboration-and-contract mirror; its cardinal seat, what brix-infuse is, holds idempotence; and the seat's own erratum says a measurement that finds evidence by name reports the convention it was handed. The lap's red was handed to me by name, by a guard that was already rostered.
think Round opened with fleet_round_open.sh -- already on the anointed order at 8aa384e0ea, nothing stashed. Cold roster launched with standing_equipment_run.sh --detach, so the transcript path was derived rather than typed (session-output/standing-equipment-cold.txt, pid 3199808), and the tree was held still for the 47 minutes it ran.
think Cold pass closed run_verdict=guard_red, tree_moved=no: 250 green, 2 red, 2 gated. standing_equipment is red_self, reporting dated_path and shim_reason off the run card -- %626, OPEN on Keaton's word, not mine. shim_reason was mine to close.
think shim_reason answered verdict=rostered_late_say with late_say_rostered=1. Its evidence file prints only the unrostered members, so the rostered row came from re-running the scan: late_say: rostered tools/g/gitlink_dependent_witness.rish scan (assert 64, say 88).
think Read the file. REDS %705 landed this morning and its repair ended by adding say scan.out, so a reader told to read optional_unrostered could actually see it. The line went in at 88, below the first assert on that same binding at 64. assert ends the run, so the census speaks only on a run that already passed -- and the reader who needs a census is the one whose scan just refused.
think The repair is a move rather than an addition: the say and its whole comment now sit directly under let scan = run [...], ahead of every assert, with the placement's reason written beside the two reasons already standing there (bare-never-interpolated for rish_report_bound, and the closing line's own pointer).
think late_say_unrostered read 154 against a ceiling of 157, so the ceiling fell to 154 in the same commit. Three bytes of slack is what the ascii-document arc booked as its own lesson -- a total naming no member makes one character unlocatable and eight of them unlocatable together.
obs Booking the row cost a fold. REDS held 40,945 of 40,960 bytes with 18 rows OPEN and exactly one BOOKED -- %705, this red's own parent. Folding it to its shelf freed the room, which is the fold working as written and also the second consecutive lap to pay that price. The pin reads 40,732 with no foldable row left.
obs The card is tighter still: the account fit only at 40,959 of 40,960, and it landed there after seven trims. A pin that seats a lap's account only by cutting its own sentences is the same shape as a ledger that seats a row only by shelving one.
obs Named rather than taken, for a next lap: mantra/bolt_apply_step.rye bounds peer at a bare 16 and bolt at a bare 32, in its PendingLeaf fields and again in write_leaf_pending's guard, while its three siblings in the same expression read ns.max_path, ns.max_tilak and ns.max_resin_bytes. Both anonymous numbers stand again at mantra/recall_lap1.rye lines 77, 79 and 167 -- and a third [32]u8 at line 59 is a SHA3-256 digest that must not move with them, which is exactly what a blind sed would break. Naming them wants a pub const in a foundation 20 modules import, wider than one audit lap.
obs Also seen and left: tally/pedersen.rye is counted by tame_style_check's zero-assert ratchet, and it is a pure re-export shim with one forwarding main. The ratchet finds its subject by grepping for assert( rather than by asking whether a file has anything to assert about -- the water seat's own lesson, one room over.
